ve talked about a crisis of division  in this country as a president how would  you heal that well I think that's that's  why I'm running for president is we are  in the middle of a national crisis of  division and I look at what we've done  in Colorado where you know in terms of  we're almost universal health care we  did in a bipartisan way we were able to  get the oil and gas industry is sit down  with your bar metal community and  address methane regulations the first  comprehensive methane regulations in the  country  and that kind of outcome-based you know  progress really progressive achievement  is something that the country I think  needs and there's an appetite for not  people just waving their arms and  jumping around but an appetite for  people actually getting things done  would you consider a Republican vice  president I've heard you say you would  consider a woman would you consider a  Republican vice president you know I  don't I don't think that would work  and it certainly in this election I'm a  great believer once the election is over  I try to hire the most talented people I  can find from all different backgrounds  all different parts of the country and  certainly Republicans and Democrats but  if you had a Republican as a vice  president candidate that would alienate  so many Democrats would really diminish  your chances of victory you generated  some confusion when you were asked that  question by Dana Bash this week when you  were asked if you would put a woman on  the ticket and instead of just saying  yes or no you asked the question well  why isn't a woman being asked if she'd  put a male on the ticket what did you  mean by well I said I said yes I said  sure and then I said but I and what I  was trying to say however inartfully I  was trying to say that that the question  itself in some way discounts the  probability that woman will actually get  the nomination and this is such a  diverse and powerful field of candidates  it's one of the reasons I'm proud to be  a Democrat governor I want to ask you I  was talking to one of my fellow  Millennials yesterday and I said  governor Hickenlooper is gonna be on the  show he said oh yeah he was governor  when Pop became legal and he's a guy who  believes in the science behind climate  change and it made me think how do you  punch through in a crowded field you  know I had the name recognition of a lot  of the other folks so how do you rise to  the top I think when you look at  everybody I'm the one person that has  actually been out there I was a an  entrepreneur build a business from  scratch I put a team together I was  mayor for eight years and you know buy  any record we achieved we made real  progress with the city of Denver took it  from no kind of a flyover city to a to a  major emerging city and then as governor  we've really created progressive  achievement again and again I think  there is that appetite for people that  when when I got elected governor we were  40th in job creation  now we're the number one economy in the  country for the last two years according  to US News and World Report we've made  all these all this progress and  especially with Millennials when you sit  down and say here  what I can do here's we've reduced  teenage pregnancy in Colorado by 60% in  the last eight years these are the kinds  of achievements that most milania's care  about here's how we're going to create  you know opportunities and education for  everybody can we talk about the shooting  in New Zealand because just six days  after the deadliest mass shooting  they are already moving to change the  laws with little resistance there when  you look at that do you think we need to  take notes or do is there something we  can learn for that from that or do you  think that's how they do it but that's  not how we operate here or do we need to  know I think everyone should be paying  attention to how quickly they're moving  forward and when we did when we took on  the NRA back in 2013 after the shooting  in the Aurora movie is you know in the  end the NRA no matter what we said we  couldn't get them to go along with the  universal background checks even when we  pulled that to statistics it showed that  in the previous year getting to just  half the gun purchases I mean there were  I mean it was amazing there are 38  people convicted of homicide who trying  to buy a gun and we stopped them overall  over 3,000 violent criminals who tried  to buy a gun and we stopped them and yet  still we couldn't get a single  Republican to support that that  initiative would gun control be a top  priority for your administration yeah I  think we would go immediately you start  state-by-state but it should be a  national effort and ultimately a  national victory in terms of universal  background checks high-capacity  magazines these are things that don't  take away and we don't want to take away  people's guns and we're not trying to  you know diminish the Second Amendment  but we've got to make sure that we keep  guns out of the hands of dangerous you  talk about being a proud entrepreneur  why can't you just say you're a  capitalist full-stop you know it's funny  it's a little bit like a friend asked  well where you going in high school well  you know that's not the first label I  choose yeah but be hard to argue it you  know alright you're gonna put me into a  category I am a capitalist I started 20  businesses like the knobs governor  Hickenlooper his apologies we've got to  leave it there but thanks
 

